## Context

The author's idea, 2026-09-02: the indexer SHOULD use Zotero's structured
text pack when one exists, and fall back on the flat full-text cache
otherwise. `DECISIONS.md` carries it as an awaiting-ratification entry of the
same date, with the facts, the price and a recommendation. This ticket is
the work once the entry is ratified; it is labeled `needs-human` because its
first exit criterion is the author's ruling, and `Blocked-by: 0028` because
the 2026-09-01 sequencing ruling ships seg/1 first so every structure path
integrates once, on a working baseline.

What is already known, so nobody re-establishes it:

- The pack is `storage/<KEY>/.zotero-sdt-cache`, pack version 1, schema
  1.1.0, processor pdf/3. `verification/probes/sdt_read.py` reads it and
  refuses any other pack version. Two packs exist on the author's library
  against 13 630 flat caches, and only the reader writes one
  (`bench/results/0007-sdt-probe.txt`, fifth probe, trigger read at source).
- Upstream #6012 already implements pack-first, flat-fallback
  (`embeddings.js` at `77e2c4b`, `_getAttachmentChunks`) and keys the source
  on the processor version. When it ships, packs exist for every embedded
  attachment.
- The pdf.js segmenter program (tracker 0557) reaches the PDF through the
  local API's `/file/view/url` redirect; the pack is in the same directory.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §3 Corpus or Query block, the new R-item once ratified; §4 C1
  (the first derivation link gains a second extractor identity); §5.2.4 (the
  extract shim, where the pack read is decided per attachment); §5.2.2 (the
  segmenter's structure-signal interface, tier 0).
- `README.md` standing table and R24's rung roster: the new requirement must
  sit on exactly one rung or `bench/check_progress.py` fails.
- `verification/probes/sdt_read.py`: the reader to port to the fork's
  TypeScript, or the reference the port is checked against.
- `bench/results/0007-sdt-probe.txt`: the census and the trigger evidence.
- `SYNC.md` "Zotero extraction" row: the three version constants the reader
  must gate on.
- Tickets 0557, 0560, 0561 (the ladder this becomes tier 0 of), 0480
  (extractor staleness), 0483 (per-attachment truncation state), 0120 (the
  SDT branch it answers).

## Actions

1. Ratification: the author rules on the awaiting entry. Then edit `SPEC.md`
   to match (R-item, C1 link, §5.2.4 source selection, §5.2.2 tier 0) and
   `README.md` (row and roster). Bump the SPEC.md date.
2. Source selection in the extract shim: per attachment, stat the pack path
   derived from the file path the local API already yields; read it when
   present and of a known pack version; otherwise the ruled fallback, the
   local API's `/fulltext`. Never a direct read of `.zotero-ft-cache`.
3. Extractor identity: the C1 key for a pack-sourced attachment is
   (source hash, processor version) from the pack's own metadata; for a
   flat-sourced one it stays what §5.2.4 has today. Record which, per
   attachment, in the ledger.
4. Text: the pack's blocks, excluded flows dropped, joined so a passage's
   extent maps back to its blocks (what #6012's `chunkSections` contract
   does). Structure: block types and page anchors handed to the segmenter as
   a tier-0 signal above 0560's outline. Do not overlay pack structure onto
   the flat text.
5. Reporting: R17's sentence counts pack-sourced and flat-sourced
   attachments apart; R24's locator says "page" for a block anchor and keeps
   D10's "estimated" label for flat-sourced hits.
6. Regeneration under a read: write the test before the code (below), then
   make the behaviour match it.

## Test

- Red first: an attachment with a pack and a flat cache whose texts differ
  in a known way (the running head present only in the flat cache). Assert
  the indexed text is the pack's. Run it against the tree before the
  change: it must fail there.
- A pack whose version byte is bumped: assert the attachment falls back to
  the flat path and the ledger records why, and that nothing else changes.
- A processor-version change in the pack metadata with the same source hash:
  assert the next census marks the attachment stale (0480's class), and that
  a truncated or half-written pack (cut the file mid-chunk) falls back
  rather than yielding partial blocks.
- The two real packs (GMAP993G, BSMCQ6BM) as fixtures: block counts and
  heading lists match `sdt_read.py`'s output byte for byte.

## Verification

- [ ] `make check` green; the new R-item sits on exactly one rung.
- [ ] R17's report on the author's library says 2 pack-sourced, the rest
      flat, before #6012 ships; a hand count of `.zotero-sdt-cache` files
      agrees.
- [ ] A hit inside GMAP993G returns the block's page, not an estimate.

## Invariants

- seg/1 and the pdf.js tiers are never degraded: the pack is a signal above
  them, and absence of a pack changes nothing about today's path.
- No copyleft code vendored into zoteus (MIT). The reader is written from
  the format, under whatever the AGPL-reimplementation ruling decides.
- C3: reading a pack streams block groups; the pack is never held whole
  beside the query embedder.
- Nothing is filed upstream; #6012 is read, not touched.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The awaiting entry is ratified or rejected; if rejected, this ticket
      closes with the reason and nothing else changes.
- [ ] SPEC.md and README.md match the ruling; the SPEC.md date is bumped.
- [ ] Pack-first source selection lands in the fork with the four tests
      above, the red-first one having failed against the unfixed tree.
- [ ] Per-source counts appear in R17's report and a block anchor reaches
      R24's locator.
- [ ] 0557's tracker names the pack as tier 0 and 0560/0561 as the tiers
      below it.
